"Feature test: CLASS-SIDE `super` (DolphinDart DD9).

 Dolphin's universal constructor is

     Foo class >> new [ ^super new initialize ]

 and until DD9 the front-end had no path for it at all: the send fell past the
 instance-side super check (this_var_ is nil on the class side), past the
 class-NAME check (which excludes `super`), and reached TranslateVariable, which
 printed `unsupported self/super here` and yielded NULL. The method still
 compiled, so the failure surfaced later as a doesNotUnderstand on nil — never
 at the constructor. 572 sites in the dsfork corpus depend on this.

 The contract these assertions pin, in one line: RESOLUTION goes up, `self`
 does not. A class-side super send starts its lookup above the owner, but
 argument 0 stays the RECEIVING class — so an inherited constructor builds the
 subclass it was sent to, not the class that wrote it."

"── the Dolphin idiom: no ancestor defines class-side `new` ──────────────
 st/world/01_object.mst declares no class side at all, so `super new` here has
 nothing to resolve to and must mean RAW ALLOCATION of the receiving class.
 The trap avoided: re-dispatching `new` would find THIS method again and
 recurse until the stack ends."
Object subclass: SupBase [
    | tag |
    SupBase class >> new [ ^super new setTag: #base ]
    setTag: t [ tag := t ]
    tag [ ^tag ]
]

"Inherits SupBase's constructor without writing one. `SupSub new` must answer a
 SupSub — the guarded slow path, where the runtime receiving class is NOT the
 owner's instance class."
SupBase subclass: SupSub [ ]
SupSub subclass: SupSubSub [ ]

"A subclass that DOES write one, and chains through it."
SupBase subclass: SupChained [
    SupChained class >> new [ ^super new setTag: #chained ]
]

"── resolution: an ancestor DOES define the class-side selector ──────────"
Object subclass: MakeP [
    | who |
    MakeP class >> make [ ^self basicNew setWho: #P ]
    MakeP class >> make: n [ ^self basicNew setWho: n ]
    setWho: w [ who := w ]
    who [ ^who ]
]
MakeP subclass: MakeQ [
    "`super make` reaches MakeP class >> make, whose `self basicNew` must see
     self = the RECEIVING class (MakeQ), not the defining class (MakeP)."
    MakeQ class >> make [ ^super make ]
    MakeQ class >> make: n [ ^super make: n * 2 ]
    MakeQ class >> makeBasic [ ^super basicNew ]
]
MakeQ subclass: MakeR [ ]

STestCase subclass: TestClassSuper [
    tests [
        | a b c d |

        "--- the unresolved case: `super new` == allocate the receiver -------"
        a := SupBase new.
        self assert: (a isKindOf: SupBase) label: 'super new allocates at all'.
        self check: a class name is: 'SupBase'
             label: 'super new answers the class it was sent to'.
        self check: a tag is: #base
             label: 'the constructor kept running after super new'.

        "The inherited constructor: SupSub writes none, so SupBase class >> new
         runs with self = SupSub. Before DD9 this was the null."
        b := SupSub new.
        self check: b class name is: 'SupSub'
             label: 'an INHERITED super new builds the subclass, not the owner'.
        self assert: (b isKindOf: SupBase) label: 'and it is still a SupBase'.
        self check: b tag is: #base label: 'inherited constructor still ran'.

        "Two levels down — the slow path is not special-cased to one hop."
        c := SupSubSub new.
        self check: c class name is: 'SupSubSub'
             label: 'two levels of inheritance still build the receiver'.

        "A subclass with its own constructor chains INTO the inherited one:
         SupChained new -> super new -> SupBase class >> new -> super new ->
         allocate. Both bodies run, in order, and the later tag wins."
        d := SupChained new.
        self check: d class name is: 'SupChained'
             label: 'a chained constructor still builds the receiver'.
        self check: d tag is: #chained
             label: 'both constructor bodies ran, subclass last'.

        "--- the resolved case: super finds an ancestor class method ---------"
        self check: MakeQ make class name is: 'MakeQ'
             label: 'super make resolves UP but self stays the receiver'.
        self check: MakeQ make who is: #P
             label: 'and the ancestor body is the one that ran'.
        self check: MakeR make class name is: 'MakeR'
             label: 'through two levels of class-side inheritance'.

        "Arguments are evaluated and passed."
        self check: (MakeQ make: 21) who is: 42
             label: 'a class-side super send passes its argument'.

        "super basicNew, unresolved, is the same raw allocation as super new."
        self check: MakeQ makeBasic class name is: 'MakeQ'
             label: 'super basicNew allocates the receiver too'.

        "--- the recursion trap ---------------------------------------------
         If `super new` re-dispatched `new` instead of allocating, every call
         above would have blown the stack rather than answered. Reaching here
         at all is the assertion; this one states it out loud."
        self check: ((1 to: 5) inject: 0 into: [ :acc :i | acc + SupSub new tag size ])
             is: 20 label: 'repeated construction terminates (no super-new recursion)'
    ]
]
